  {
    "type": "updateEntity",
    "id": {
      "$bytes": "813a4635194545a2863d847549af7691"
    },
    "set": [
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "a126ca530c8e48d5b88882c734c38935"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Saga year-two identification and bound assumptions",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "e3e363d1dd294ccb8e6ff3b76d99bc33"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "## What Table 6 identifies\n\nStudy 1 randomized the original tutoring offer, not each additional year of participation separately. First-stage estimates describe participation responses to that offer. The academic model estimates a weighted effect per year, observed in the second postrandomization school year.\n\n## Bounds and assumptions\n\nThe authors bound persistent first-year and two-year-participation benefits by assuming both benefits are nonnegative and setting the other component to zero. These are identification bounds, not confidence limits or separate point effects. A negative course-failure bound preserves the reported beneficial reduction direction; it is not an ordinary upper numerical endpoint. Each reported standard error describes its estimator; the FDR q-values can differ across specifications.\n\n## Comparison limits\n\nDo not combine participation first stages, per-year mixtures and bounds as interchangeable academic effects. The graph stores bounds in a distinct numeric property. All records refer to the same Study 1, and add no independent experiment.\n\n## Source\n\n[Final AER article](https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.20210434), Table 6 on printed page 753, with identification discussion on pages 751–752.",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "unset": []
  },
